      "body": "This post outlines a hypothetical experiment to understand the relationship between context length and VRAM consumption in local LLMs. The core scenario is that a specific model, runtime, and hardware configuration are held constant throughout the tests. The objective is to observe memory usage at different context lengths without collecting any data on output quality or inference speed.\n\nSpecifically, the hypothetical data suggests that a context length of 2048 tokens might require approximately 7 GB of VRAM, while extending this to 8192 tokens could increase the requirement to around 11 GB. It's crucial to reiterate that these are purely hypothetical figures from a planned, not executed, experiment. No actual benchmarks or performance metrics were gathered.\n\nThis is intended to frame a discussion on resource planning for larger context windows. What are others' experiences with memory scaling, even if qualitative, when exploring longer contexts?",
